    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-344287",
      "knowledge_point_id": "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials",
      "problem": "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+1$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-30x+24.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-30x+24=0\\,⟹\\,x=1,\\,4.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2,5$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =6(0)^{2}-30(0)+24=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2)^{2}-30(2)+24=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =6(5)^{2}-30(5)+24=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |  | $5$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=4$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=4.$",
      "answer_fields": [
