    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-22501",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions",
      "problem": "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=x{e}^{x}$ have a local maximum?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}=(1+x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 1+x & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =(1+(-2)){e}^{-2}=-{e}^{-2}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(1+0){e}^{0}=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ does not have any local maxima.",
